1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,832
The brush uncovers a square of soft stone, barely bigger than a postage stamp.

2
00:00:05,832 --> 00:00:10,939
On it, an animal in profile with a single horn, and above it a row of signs.

3
00:00:15,333 --> 00:00:17,487
Thousands of these have been found.

4
00:00:17,487 --> 00:00:19,761
We can see every one of them clearly.

5
00:00:19,761 --> 00:00:21,796
We cannot read a single one.

6
00:00:25,667 --> 00:00:27,584
So the same rule as before holds.

7
00:00:27,584 --> 00:00:32,772
We know Harappan life from what was dug up — and from one tantalising source we cannot use,

8
00:00:32,772 --> 00:00:36,568
because the writing on these seals has never been deciphered.

9
00:00:42,633 --> 00:00:46,024
Start with the land, because everything rests on it.

10
00:00:46,024 --> 00:00:50,886
Harappans grew wheat and barley above all, and also peas, sesame and dates.

11
00:00:50,886 --> 00:00:55,281
They grew cotton — among the earliest people anywhere to do so.

12
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:06,616
At Kalibangan, archaeologists found a ploughed field, the furrows still crossing at right angles.

13
00:01:06,616 --> 00:01:09,706
They kept cattle, sheep, goats and buffalo.

14
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:17,710
Then crafts, and these people were superb at them.

15
00:01:17,710 --> 00:01:22,057
Beads of carnelian, drilled with a precision that still impresses.

16
00:01:22,057 --> 00:01:23,915
Bangles cut from shell.

17
00:01:27,767 --> 00:01:29,954
Copper and bronze tools and vessels.

18
00:01:29,954 --> 00:01:32,555
Pottery thrown on the wheel and fired hard.

19
00:01:32,555 --> 00:01:37,643
Chanhudaro seems to have been a town of workshops rather than a city of its own.

20
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:45,111
Now the part that shows what kind of society this was: weights.

21
00:01:45,111 --> 00:01:52,758
Harappan weights are cubes of chert, and they run in a doubling series — one, two, four, eight, sixteen and on upwards.

22
00:01:52,758 --> 00:01:56,244
The same weights turn up across the whole region.

23
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,544
That means standards, and standards mean trade someone is checking.

24
00:02:04,544 --> 00:02:10,751
Harappan goods reached Mesopotamia, and Mesopotamian texts mention trade with a place they called Meluhha,

25
00:02:10,751 --> 00:02:13,893
which most scholars take to be the Indus region.

26
00:02:20,467 --> 00:02:21,372
And the seals.

27
00:02:21,372 --> 00:02:26,443
Made of steatite, carved with an animal and a short line of signs, then fired hard.

28
00:02:26,443 --> 00:02:32,658
Pressed into wet clay, a seal leaves a mark — so they were probably used to seal and label goods.

29
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,770
The script runs mostly right to left, there are something over four hundred distinct signs, and no bilingual text has ever been found.

30
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:49,005
Without one, decipherment is extraordinarily hard.

31
00:02:51,967 --> 00:02:56,078
Harappan religion is where confidence runs furthest ahead of evidence.

32
00:02:56,078 --> 00:02:59,785
Some scholars point to seals showing a seated figure, to trees,

33
00:02:59,785 --> 00:03:05,239
animals and fire altars, and see the beginnings of ideas found in later Indian religion.

34
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:18,350
Others reply that with no readable text, naming any figure on a seal is interpretation rather than evidence.

35
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:24,349
Both positions are held by serious people, and this series will not pretend the question is settled.

36
00:03:30,633 --> 00:03:33,496
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

37
00:03:33,496 --> 00:03:36,219
Three words for how this world worked.

38
00:03:36,219 --> 00:03:37,845
Seal, scale, sail.

39
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:45,491
Dig Quiz.

40
00:03:45,491 --> 00:03:46,804
Pause and name it.

41
00:03:46,804 --> 00:03:52,907
First: a field with furrows crossing at right angles, preserved under later layers.

42
00:03:57,667 --> 00:04:00,095
The ploughed field at Kalibangan.

43
00:04:00,095 --> 00:04:04,523
Second: a cube of stone, one of a set that doubles in weight.

44
00:04:04,523 --> 00:04:06,179
A Harappan weight.

45
00:04:10,333 --> 00:04:13,895
Third: a long bead of carnelian, drilled end to end.

46
00:04:13,895 --> 00:04:15,172
A Chanhudaro bead.

47
00:04:15,172 --> 00:04:21,788
In Mains, a frequent question asks what Harappan weights and seals tell us about the economy.

48
00:04:27,667 --> 00:04:33,052
Use them as evidence of standardisation, of supervision, and of trade across long distances.

49
00:04:36,433 --> 00:04:43,029
The seal takes its place in the gallery: the clearest thing the Harappans left us, and the one we still cannot read.

50
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:51,589
Next gallery: the great sites, and why this world came to an end.

51
00:04:51,589 --> 00:04:55,594
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

